<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atiedian Brethren</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fratres Atiedii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Atiedian Brethren (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> fratres Atiedii) were a college of priests at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gubbio">Iguvium</a>, modern Gubbio, in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>. Their rituals, including the purification of the city's sacred mount, sacrifices and the reading of bird signs, are recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iguvine-tablets">Iguvine Tablets</a> of the 3rd to 1st centuries BCE. These texts make them the best documented priesthood of any ancient Italic people other than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p></div>
